1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,594
In 1822, the Sanchi stupa was cut open in search of treasure.

2
00:00:03,594 --> 00:00:07,547
An English officer had digging started from the top of the dome.

3
00:00:07,547 --> 00:00:11,501
He thought gold and silver must be hidden inside. The workers kept removing stones,

4
00:00:11,501 --> 00:00:12,938
and the pit kept getting deeper.

5
00:00:12,938 --> 00:00:15,814
But he never managed to reach the center.

6
00:00:15,814 --> 00:00:17,970
And he left the work unfinished.

7
00:00:17,970 --> 00:00:21,582
That officer's name was Herbert Maddock, a British political agent.

8
00:00:21,582 --> 00:00:24,866
Alexander Cunningham recorded this in his book.

9
00:00:24,866 --> 00:00:27,821
A big wound was left in the dome, and the stones were scattered.

10
00:00:27,821 --> 00:00:29,791
A stupa that had stood for centuries

11
00:00:29,791 --> 00:00:31,761
was hurt by one man's greed.

12
00:00:31,761 --> 00:00:34,060
And this is where the question begins.

13
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:37,093
Were there Buddha's relics in the middle of this stupa?

14
00:00:37,093 --> 00:00:39,216
Relics means the bones left after cremation,

15
00:00:39,216 --> 00:00:40,732
which are considered sacred.

16
00:00:40,732 --> 00:00:44,068
Or was this dome built without any relics at all?

17
00:00:44,068 --> 00:00:46,191
To this day, nobody knows for sure.

18
00:00:46,191 --> 00:00:48,617
In this video we will go step by step with the evidence,

19
00:00:48,617 --> 00:00:49,830
opening it up one layer at a time.

20
00:00:49,830 --> 00:00:53,954
This is the story of a hill, of Sanchi in Madhya Pradesh.

21
00:00:53,954 --> 00:00:56,203
First we will see what the texts say.

22
00:00:56,203 --> 00:00:59,577
Then we will see what the excavation found. And finally,

23
00:00:59,577 --> 00:01:01,827
where the scholars disagree with each other.

24
00:01:01,827 --> 00:01:05,201
On every panel you will see a chip. It will tell you

25
00:01:05,201 --> 00:01:08,200
whether this is a text, evidence, or just a story.

26
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:12,233
First understand what a stupa is. A stupa is a round dome

27
00:01:12,233 --> 00:01:15,899
inside which sacred relics are kept. According to UNESCO,

28
00:01:15,899 --> 00:01:18,831
the great stupa of Sanchi was built by Emperor Ashoka.

29
00:01:18,831 --> 00:01:21,398
This dates to the 3rd century BCE,

30
00:01:21,398 --> 00:01:23,597
that is, centuries before Christ.

31
00:01:23,597 --> 00:01:26,530
In the beginning, it was a dome made of bricks.

32
00:01:26,530 --> 00:01:28,291
Now let's turn to the texts.

33
00:01:28,291 --> 00:01:31,109
There is an old text from Sri Lanka, the Mahavamsa.

34
00:01:31,109 --> 00:01:34,983
It records the history of the kings and Buddhism there.

35
00:01:34,983 --> 00:01:36,744
It has a love story in it.

36
00:01:36,744 --> 00:01:39,914
Young Ashoka stopped in the city of Vidisha on a journey.

37
00:01:39,914 --> 00:01:42,380
He stayed as a guest at a merchant's house.

38
00:01:42,380 --> 00:01:43,553
The Mahavamsa says

39
00:01:43,553 --> 00:01:47,070
that there Ashoka married the merchant's daughter.

40
00:01:47,070 --> 00:01:51,370
Her name was Devi, and the text calls her a woman of good family. Vidisha,

41
00:01:51,370 --> 00:01:55,278
It is a town very close to Sanchi. According to the text,

42
00:01:55,278 --> 00:01:58,014
Devi had a monastery built on Vedisagiri.

43
00:01:58,014 --> 00:02:00,750
A monastery means a place where monks live.

44
00:02:00,750 --> 00:02:04,918
The Vedisagiri of the text is believed to be near today's Sanchi.

45
00:02:04,918 --> 00:02:09,087
So the story has a king, and a merchant's daughter.

46
00:02:09,087 --> 00:02:12,497
And a hill where peace lived. Imagine,

47
00:02:12,497 --> 00:02:16,286
a very old evening, lamps burning on the hill.

48
00:02:16,286 --> 00:02:18,560
The monks sit quietly in meditation.

49
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:21,221
A folk tale is connected to this place.

50
00:02:21,221 --> 00:02:25,023
The village people say, this is why Ashok chose Sanchi.

51
00:02:25,023 --> 00:02:29,205
Because this was the hill where he married Devi.

52
00:02:29,205 --> 00:02:32,247
But keep in mind, this is only a folk tale.

53
00:02:32,247 --> 00:02:36,049
No inscription from the Maurya period shows Devi here.

54
00:02:36,049 --> 00:02:38,330
An inscription means a message carved on stone.

55
00:02:38,330 --> 00:02:40,711
So the text tells a love story.

56
00:02:40,711 --> 00:02:43,773
The folk tale turns it into the place of the wedding.

57
00:02:43,773 --> 00:02:47,175
And the stones are still silent. Remember this difference,

58
00:02:47,175 --> 00:02:50,237
it will be useful throughout the video. Now another text,

59
00:02:50,237 --> 00:02:53,639
which makes another big claim. A number

60
00:02:53,639 --> 00:02:55,340
that makes your head spin when you hear it.

61
00:02:55,340 --> 00:02:59,257
This text is the Ashokavadana, the story of Ashok written in Sanskrit.

62
00:02:59,257 --> 00:03:03,173
Avadana means a story of good deeds. It says that

63
00:03:03,173 --> 00:03:05,915
Ashok had the relics of the Buddha collected.

64
00:03:05,915 --> 00:03:09,440
These relics were kept in 7 old stupas.

65
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:11,790
Ashok had those stupas opened.

66
00:03:11,790 --> 00:03:14,643
Then the text says, boxes of gold, silver,

67
00:03:14,643 --> 00:03:17,973
crystal and lapis lazuli were made.

68
00:03:17,973 --> 00:03:21,302
Lapis lazuli means a deep blue precious stone.

69
00:03:21,302 --> 00:03:24,631
The relics were divided among these boxes.

70
00:03:24,631 --> 00:03:28,911
And 84,000 stupas were built across the whole empire. Imagine,

71
00:03:28,911 --> 00:03:32,240
just one king, and so many stupas.

72
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:34,789
Remember, this is what the text says,

73
00:03:34,789 --> 00:03:36,974
that is why the chip says scripture.

74
00:03:36,974 --> 00:03:39,158
The text shows a grand picture.

75
00:03:39,158 --> 00:03:43,164
But proof of the number has to be found outside the text. Yes,

76
00:03:43,164 --> 00:03:47,897
the old brick dome of Sanchi is believed to be from Ashok's time.

77
00:03:47,897 --> 00:03:50,810
Here the text and the stone meet in one place.

78
00:03:50,810 --> 00:03:54,603
Now the question is, how was such a big stupa lost?

79
00:03:54,603 --> 00:03:58,016
Because at one time people forgot Sanchi.

80
00:03:58,016 --> 00:04:01,051
Bushes grew on the hill, and stones fell down.

81
00:04:01,051 --> 00:04:03,706
And then came an English cavalry officer.

82
00:04:03,706 --> 00:04:06,740
This is where the story of the discovery begins.

83
00:04:06,740 --> 00:04:09,000
ASI, that is, the Archaeological Survey of India.

84
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,521
This government body looks after old heritage sites. According to the ASI,

85
00:04:13,521 --> 00:04:18,493
Sanchi lay deserted for centuries. No one came to worship,

86
00:04:18,493 --> 00:04:23,466
and no one looked after it. Then in 1818 General Taylor arrived here.

87
00:04:23,466 --> 00:04:26,630
He was an officer in the British cavalry.

88
00:04:26,630 --> 00:04:27,021
Just imagine,

89
00:04:27,021 --> 00:04:31,321
an officer on horseback pushes aside the bushes and climbs up.

90
00:04:31,321 --> 00:04:34,840
In front is a huge round dome, half buried in soil.

91
00:04:34,840 --> 00:04:37,576
Around it are broken pillars and carved gateways.

92
00:04:37,576 --> 00:04:40,313
Taylor recorded this place.

93
00:04:40,313 --> 00:04:43,440
And the world's eyes turned to Sanchi again.

94
00:04:43,440 --> 00:04:46,483
But being noticed is not always a good thing.

95
00:04:46,483 --> 00:04:51,266
Some Englishmen came who did not want history, they wanted treasure.

96
00:04:51,266 --> 00:04:55,614
And then came 1822, which we mentioned at the start.

97
00:04:55,614 --> 00:04:59,961
Herbert Maddock had a deep dig made into the dome from the top.

98
00:04:59,961 --> 00:05:02,570
The aim was to take out the treasure hidden inside.

99
00:05:02,570 --> 00:05:05,975
Cunningham later wrote that this digging was done in a clumsy way.

100
00:05:05,975 --> 00:05:08,142
A big gash was made in the dome.

101
00:05:08,142 --> 00:05:11,238
The outer layer of stone also collapsed in many places.

102
00:05:11,238 --> 00:05:14,643
And the strangest thing is, Maddock never even reached the center.

103
00:05:14,643 --> 00:05:16,500
He left the work unfinished and went away.

104
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:19,712
Now think, if there really were relics in the middle.

105
00:05:19,712 --> 00:05:22,567
Then did they get scattered in this digging?

106
00:05:22,567 --> 00:05:25,422
Or are they still inside the dome today?

107
00:05:25,422 --> 00:05:27,920
Or was there never anything there at all?

108
00:05:27,920 --> 00:05:31,489
We will raise these questions again at the end. First let's see

109
00:05:31,489 --> 00:05:33,630
how the broken stupa stood up again.

110
00:05:33,630 --> 00:05:36,382
A broken stupa, scattered stones, and jungle.

111
00:05:36,382 --> 00:05:39,526
For many years Sanchi lay like this.

112
00:05:39,526 --> 00:05:44,636
Then an English archaeologist and a begum of Bhopal took up the work together.

113
00:05:44,636 --> 00:05:48,960
This is the most comforting part of this story. See how.

114
00:05:48,960 --> 00:05:51,339
His name was Sir John Marshall,

115
00:05:51,339 --> 00:05:54,510
at that time the head of the archaeology department in India.

116
00:05:54,510 --> 00:05:58,078
Archaeology means the science of old things found in the ground.

117
00:05:58,078 --> 00:06:02,042
In 1912 Marshall started systematic work at Sanchi.

118
00:06:02,042 --> 00:06:05,610
Systematic means measuring carefully, keeping records, step by step.

119
00:06:05,610 --> 00:06:09,326
The money for this work was given by Sultan Jahan Begum of Bhopal.

120
00:06:09,326 --> 00:06:12,704
A ruler opened her treasure to save her heritage.

121
00:06:12,704 --> 00:06:14,731
Now look at this reversal.

122
00:06:14,731 --> 00:06:17,095
One broke the stupa for treasure.

123
00:06:17,095 --> 00:06:19,460
The other gave treasure for the stupa.

124
00:06:19,460 --> 00:06:23,641
Marshall's team identified the fallen stones one by one.

125
00:06:23,641 --> 00:06:27,125
They joined the broken pieces and made the dome strong again.

126
00:06:27,125 --> 00:06:29,912
What was scattered was put back in its place.

127
00:06:29,912 --> 00:06:33,396
Conservation means protecting what is left from falling any further.

128
00:06:33,396 --> 00:06:34,790
This work went on for years.

129
00:06:34,790 --> 00:06:38,654
And in 1919, Marshall also built an archaeological museum here.

130
00:06:38,654 --> 00:06:39,707
According to the ASI,

131
00:06:39,707 --> 00:06:42,869
that same museum later became today's Sanchi site museum.

132
00:06:42,869 --> 00:06:46,732
So to see the story of Sanchi, you don't have to go far.

133
00:06:46,732 --> 00:06:48,840
This point will be useful at the end.

134
00:06:48,840 --> 00:06:51,111
Now look carefully at the stupa.

135
00:06:51,111 --> 00:06:54,518
Inside is a brick dome from Ashoka's time.

136
00:06:54,518 --> 00:06:58,682
Later, a thick layer of stone was added over it.

137
00:06:58,682 --> 00:07:02,467
According to UNESCO, this greatly increased the stupa's width.

138
00:07:02,467 --> 00:07:04,739
Like putting not just plaster on an old house,

139
00:07:04,739 --> 00:07:07,010
but a whole new wall over it.

140
00:07:07,010 --> 00:07:09,739
Today its diameter is 36.6 metres.

141
00:07:09,739 --> 00:07:14,741
Diameter means the straight distance from one edge to the other.

142
00:07:14,741 --> 00:07:18,379
And the dome's height is about 16.5 metres.

143
00:07:18,379 --> 00:07:22,927
That is as tall as a tall building. A dome this big, without cement,

144
00:07:22,927 --> 00:07:27,020
without machines. Just think how many hands carried the stones.

145
00:07:27,020 --> 00:07:30,034
Now look at the gates around the stupa.

146
00:07:30,034 --> 00:07:33,801
These are called toranas, meaning the main gate made of carved stone.

147
00:07:33,801 --> 00:07:37,945
Scenes from stories are carved on them: elephants, trees, kings, cities.

148
00:07:37,945 --> 00:07:41,336
On the southern gate there is an inscription in Brahmi script.

149
00:07:41,336 --> 00:07:44,350
Brahmi means a very old script of India.

150
00:07:44,350 --> 00:07:47,246
That inscription tells something surprising.

151
00:07:47,246 --> 00:07:51,227
This carving was done by the ivory workers of Vidisha.

152
00:07:51,227 --> 00:07:55,209
People who did fine work on ivory, now working on stone.

153
00:07:55,209 --> 00:07:59,190
Maybe that is why this carving looks so delicate. As if it isn't stone,

154
00:07:59,190 --> 00:08:01,000
as if someone had carved tusk.

155
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:04,359
Near the southern gate stands one more proof,

156
00:08:04,359 --> 00:08:08,465
Ashoka's pillar. An edict of Ashoka is carved on it.

157
00:08:08,465 --> 00:08:12,571
An edict means a king's written order, meant for everyone.

158
00:08:12,571 --> 00:08:13,691
According to the ASI,

159
00:08:13,691 --> 00:08:17,050
this order is a warning against division in the Sangha.

160
00:08:17,050 --> 00:08:21,104
Sangha means the community of monks and nuns. The order says,

161
00:08:21,104 --> 00:08:25,564
Any monk or nun who breaks the Sangha will be expelled.

162
00:08:25,564 --> 00:08:30,024
Imagine an emperor having words carved on stone: keep the unity.

163
00:08:30,024 --> 00:08:35,700
This is the place where both the texts and the inscriptions name Ashoka.

164
00:08:35,700 --> 00:08:38,425
Now we come to the most interesting debate.

165
00:08:38,425 --> 00:08:41,539
There is something strange in the carvings on the gateways.

166
00:08:41,539 --> 00:08:45,042
In many scenes, the body of the Buddha is not shown at all.

167
00:08:45,042 --> 00:08:49,324
In its place there are only symbols. An empty throne, a horse with no rider,

168
00:08:49,324 --> 00:08:51,270
footprints, a wheel.

169
00:08:51,270 --> 00:08:55,605
The scholar Alfred Foucher said this was done on purpose.

170
00:08:55,605 --> 00:08:59,547
According to him, there was a religious ban on showing the Buddha's body.

171
00:08:59,547 --> 00:09:01,912
So the artists made only symbols.

172
00:09:01,912 --> 00:09:04,276
This idea is called aniconism.

173
00:09:04,276 --> 00:09:07,823
Aniconism means showing the object of worship not as a statue,

174
00:09:07,823 --> 00:09:09,400
but only through a symbol.

175
00:09:09,400 --> 00:09:11,423
Then came the art historian Susan Huntington.

176
00:09:11,423 --> 00:09:14,796
She put forward her view in an article in the Art Journal.

177
00:09:14,796 --> 00:09:15,807
She said,

178
00:09:15,807 --> 00:09:18,843
these are not scenes from the life of the Buddha at all.

179
00:09:18,843 --> 00:09:20,866
These are people of that time,

180
00:09:20,866 --> 00:09:23,227
who came to worship at sacred places.

181
00:09:23,227 --> 00:09:25,250
That is, a scene of pilgrimage carved in stone.

182
00:09:25,250 --> 00:09:28,825
So one panel, two different readings. Foucher says,

183
00:09:28,825 --> 00:09:32,400
the Buddha is here, just not shown. Huntington says,

184
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:35,975
these are devotees, worshipping a sacred place.

185
00:09:35,975 --> 00:09:39,193
Both are big scholars, and both have arguments.

186
00:09:39,193 --> 00:09:40,980
The matter is still open today.

187
00:09:40,980 --> 00:09:44,253
Now let's go back to the question we started with.

188
00:09:44,253 --> 00:09:47,228
Were the Buddha's bones inside the big stupa?

189
00:09:47,228 --> 00:09:49,905
The texts say Ashoka divided the relics among the stupas.

190
00:09:49,905 --> 00:09:54,070
But from the middle of the big stupa, no such proof is in front of us today.

191
00:09:54,070 --> 00:09:57,721
There are two possibilities, and both are open. First, the bones were there,

192
00:09:57,721 --> 00:10:01,007
and were lost in the amateur digs of the British era.

193
00:10:01,007 --> 00:10:05,023
Amateur means digging without training, only out of hobby or greed.

194
00:10:05,023 --> 00:10:09,039
Second, this dome was built without any bodily remains at all.

195
00:10:09,039 --> 00:10:11,960
Maybe it was a memorial of remembrance and devotion.

196
00:10:11,960 --> 00:10:13,115
One more thing.

197
00:10:13,115 --> 00:10:18,892
In the smaller stupas nearby, boxes with remains of the Buddha's disciples have been found.

198
00:10:18,892 --> 00:10:20,818
Disciples means his close followers.

199
00:10:20,818 --> 00:10:24,284
So there was a tradition of keeping relics on this hill,

200
00:10:24,284 --> 00:10:25,439
that much is clear.

201
00:10:25,439 --> 00:10:29,290
Only the heart of the big stupa is still a mystery.

202
00:10:29,290 --> 00:10:32,468
So if you visit Sanchi, what should you see?

203
00:10:32,468 --> 00:10:36,043
This place is in Madhya Pradesh, near Vidisha.

204
00:10:36,043 --> 00:10:41,207
UNESCO has put it on the list of World Heritage, that is, the world's heritage sites.

205
00:10:41,207 --> 00:10:45,180
It means the whole world considers it the shared heritage of humanity.

206
00:10:45,180 --> 00:10:48,904
First, the big stupa, and the carved gateways near it.

207
00:10:48,904 --> 00:10:52,257
On the southern gateway, look for that Brahmi inscription, the one by the craftsmen.

208
00:10:52,257 --> 00:10:55,609
Nearby, also see the spot of Ashoka's pillar.

209
00:10:55,609 --> 00:10:59,333
Look carefully at the scenes on the gateways. If you see an empty throne,

210
00:10:59,333 --> 00:11:03,430
then decide for yourself. Are you with Foucher or with Huntington?

211
00:11:03,430 --> 00:11:07,454
Then do visit the site museum, whose foundation was laid by Marshall.

212
00:11:07,454 --> 00:11:10,380
There you will see the excavated items up close.

213
00:11:10,380 --> 00:11:12,575
And pause for a moment and think.

214
00:11:12,575 --> 00:11:14,770
An Englishman searched for treasure here,

215
00:11:14,770 --> 00:11:17,697
a begum put treasure here. The same hill,

216
00:11:17,697 --> 00:11:19,160
two completely different intentions.

217
00:11:19,160 --> 00:11:22,980
Now it's your turn. We looked at the texts, heard the folk tale,

218
00:11:22,980 --> 00:11:27,649
and saw the evidence from the excavations. We saw where scholars disagree.

219
00:11:27,649 --> 00:11:31,894
Now the question is for you, and the answer depends on your own understanding.

220
00:11:31,894 --> 00:11:35,290
There is no right or wrong, just your own thinking.

221
00:11:35,290 --> 00:11:41,489
Next time we will go under the sea, in search of Dwarka.

222
00:11:41,489 --> 00:11:43,179
There are divers there,

223
00:11:43,179 --> 00:11:48,251
that is, people who go down into the water to search.

224
00:11:48,251 --> 00:11:52,196
There too, there are texts and debates.

225
00:11:52,196 --> 00:11:57,267
Until then, tell us in the comments. What do you think,

226
00:11:57,267 --> 00:12:03,466
were there Buddha's relics inside the Sanchi stupa or not?
